// Rep Max questions: Microeconomics (Themes 1 and 3), Pearson Edexcel A level Economics A (9EC0). One question per line.
// Fields: id, s, t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest), q, o (four options), a (index 0-3), x (explanation), w (why each wrong option is wrong).
// Written by Sonnet 5.5, checked by Sonnet 5.5, flagged items settled by Opus 5.5 (provenance FNF_RepMax_E1_*, E2_*).
{"id": "econ-001", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.3", "lv": 1, "q": "A firm raises its price by 10% and the quantity demanded falls by 15%. What is the price elasticity of demand?", "o": ["−0.67", "+1.5", "−25", "−1.5"], "a": 3, "x": "PED = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔP = −15 ÷ 10 = −1.5. Price up, quantity down, so the sign is negative. Demand is elastic because the value is beyond −1 (magnitude above 1).", "w": ["You divided %ΔP by %ΔQd; the formula has quantity demanded on top.", "Dropped the sign. Price rises and quantity falls, so PED is negative.", "You added the percentages instead of dividing them.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-002", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.3", "lv": 1, "q": "The income elasticity of demand for own-brand bus tickets is −0.4. What does this tell you about the good?", "o": ["It is a normal necessity with income-inelastic demand", "It is an inferior good", "It is a normal good with income-elastic demand", "It is a luxury good"], "a": 1, "x": "A negative YED means demand falls as income rises, so the good is inferior. Size only shows how responsive demand is; the sign gives the type. Normal goods have positive YED.", "w": ["A necessity has a positive YED between 0 and 1; the negative sign means inferior.", "", "Normal goods have positive YED; a negative value cannot be normal, and −0.4 is not elastic anyway.", "Luxuries have YED above +1; this value is negative."]}
{"id": "econ-003", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Monthly income rises from £2,000 to £2,200. A household's restaurant meals rise from 10 to 13 a month. What is YED?", "o": ["+30", "+3.0", "+1.3", "+0.33"], "a": 1, "x": "%ΔY = 200/2,000 = 10%. %ΔQ = 3/10 = 30%. YED = 30 ÷ 10 = +3.0. Positive and above 1, so a normal, income-elastic (luxury-type) good.", "w": ["That is the %ΔQ alone; you forgot to divide by %ΔY.", "", "You divided 13 by 10 using the raw quantities rather than percentage changes.", "You inverted the formula: income goes on the bottom, not the top."]}
{"id": "econ-004", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.3", "lv": 2, "q": "The price of tea rises from £2.00 to £2.50 and the quantity of coffee demanded rises from 400 to 440. What is XED of coffee with respect to tea?", "o": ["+0.4; complements", "−0.4; weak complements", "+0.4; weak substitutes", "+2.5; close substitutes"], "a": 2, "x": "%ΔP of tea = 25%. %ΔQ of coffee = 40/400 = 10%. XED = 10 ÷ 25 = +0.4. Positive means substitutes; a small value means a weak link.", "w": ["The value is right, but a positive XED means substitutes, not complements.", "Wrong sign: coffee demand rose when tea's price rose, so XED is positive.", "", "You inverted the ratio; the change in demand for coffee goes on top."]}
{"id": "econ-005", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.3", "lv": 2, "q": "A firm's product has PED of −0.5. It raises price by 8%. What happens to its total revenue?", "o": ["Stays the same, because the changes cancel out", "Rises, because demand is price elastic", "Falls, because quantity demanded falls", "Rises, because quantity falls proportionately less than price rises"], "a": 3, "x": "Quantity falls about 4% (0.5 × 8%), less than the 8% rise in price. Demand is inelastic, so revenue (P × Q) rises. Revenue only falls from a price rise if demand is elastic.", "w": ["Revenue only stays the same at unit elasticity (PED = −1), not −0.5.", "The conclusion is right but the reason is wrong: −0.5 is inelastic, not elastic.", "Quantity does fall, but by less than price rises, so revenue rises.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-006", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.3", "lv": 3, "q": "PED is −0.5. A good sells 1,000 units at £4. A tax raises the price to £4.40. Assuming the PED applies, what happens to consumer spending (revenue)?", "o": ["Rises by £400 to £4,400", "Is unchanged at £4,000", "Rises by £180 to £4,180", "Falls by £200 to £3,800"], "a": 2, "x": "%ΔP = 10%, so %ΔQ = −5%: Q = 950. New spending = £4.40 × 950 = £4,180, against £4,000 before. A rise of £180, because demand is inelastic.", "w": ["You applied the new price to the old quantity; quantity falls 5% to 950 units.", "Spending is unchanged only if PED = −1; here demand is inelastic.", "", "£3,800 is £4 × 950: you cut quantity to 950 but kept the old price of £4, ignoring the rise to £4.40."]}
{"id": "econ-007", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.9", "lv": 1, "q": "On a supply and demand diagram, how is a per-unit indirect tax of £2 shown?", "o": ["Both curves pivot to become steeper by £2", "Supply shifts right by £2 at every quantity", "Demand shifts left by £2 at every price", "Supply shifts vertically upwards by £2 at every quantity"], "a": 3, "x": "A per-unit tax raises firms' costs by £2 on each unit, so the supply curve shifts vertically up by the tax. Demand does not move, because the tax is levied on firms, not on buyers' tastes or incomes.", "w": ["A per-unit tax gives a parallel shift of supply. Pivoting is for ad valorem taxes, and neither curve changes in this way here.", "A rightward shift would show a subsidy or lower costs, not a tax. A tax moves supply upwards or leftwards.", "Demand is not the curve affected; the tax raises producers' costs, so it is the supply curve that shifts.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-008", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.9", "lv": 1, "q": "After a per-unit tax, which area shows the burden of the tax borne by consumers?", "o": ["Rectangle between the old and new market price, up to the old quantity", "Rectangle between the new market price and the supply curve before tax", "Rectangle between the old and new market price, up to the new quantity", "The whole tax rectangle between the two supply curves"], "a": 2, "x": "Consumers' burden is the rise in the price they pay, from the old price to the new price, multiplied by the new quantity sold. Using the old quantity is the common slip.", "w": ["The old quantity is no longer sold after the tax. Use the new, lower quantity for every incidence area.", "This does not show the price rise consumers face; the area is bounded by the old and new market prices.", "", "This is the total tax revenue, shared between consumers and producers, not the consumers' burden alone."]}
{"id": "econ-009", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.9", "lv": 2, "q": "A £2 per-unit tax raises the market price from £5.00 to £6.50. Quantity falls from 1,000 to 800 units. What is the burden of the tax on producers?", "o": ["£400", "£500", "£1,600", "£1,200"], "a": 0, "x": "Consumers pay £1.50 more per unit, so producers bear £2.00 − £1.50 = £0.50 per unit. Multiply by the new quantity: £0.50 × 800 = £400. Using 1,000 gives £500, which is the old-quantity trap.", "w": ["", "This uses the old quantity of 1,000. Tax is only paid on units still sold, which is 800.", "This is total tax revenue (£2 × 800), not the producers' share.", "This is the consumers' burden (£1.50 × 800), not the producers'."]}
{"id": "econ-010", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.9", "lv": 2, "q": "A government taxes a good with price inelastic demand and price elastic supply. Which outcome is most likely?", "o": ["Consumers bear most of the tax and quantity falls only slightly", "Consumers bear a small share because they can switch away", "Producers bear most of the tax and quantity falls sharply", "Both groups share the tax equally whatever the elasticities"], "a": 0, "x": "Inelastic demand means buyers keep buying when price rises, so firms pass most of the tax on. Elastic supply means firms can cut output easily. Consumers bear more, and quantity falls only a little.", "w": ["", "Switching away needs elastic demand. Here demand is inelastic, so consumers bear a large share.", "This reverses the effect. Firms pass on the tax when buyers are unresponsive, so producers do not bear most.", "The split depends on the relative elasticities. It is equal only in special cases."]}
{"id": "econ-011", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.9", "lv": 2, "q": "A £2 per-unit subsidy increases quantity from 500 to 600 units and lowers the market price from £10.00 to £9.20. What is the cost to the government?", "o": ["£1,200", "£1,000", "£480", "£1,100"], "a": 0, "x": "Subsidy spending is the subsidy per unit times the new quantity: £2 × 600 = £1,200. The old quantity of 500 is the trap. The price fall of £0.80 only gives the consumers' gain.", "w": ["", "This uses the old quantity of 500. The subsidy is paid on every unit now produced, 600.", "This is the consumers' gain (£0.80 × 600), not the whole cost to the government.", "This has no basis in the data. The cost is the subsidy per unit times the new quantity sold."]}
{"id": "econ-012", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.9", "lv": 3, "q": "Demand for a good is perfectly inelastic at 200 units. A £3 per-unit tax is imposed and supply is upward sloping. What is the result?", "o": ["Price rises by £3, consumers bear £600, producers bear nothing", "Price is unchanged and producers bear the full £600", "Price rises by less than £3 and quantity falls", "Price rises by £3 and tax revenue is zero"], "a": 0, "x": "With perfectly inelastic demand, quantity stays 200, so the supply shift raises price by the full £3. Consumers bear £3 × 200 = £600 and producers bear none. Revenue is also £600.", "w": ["", "Producers can only bear the tax if buyers will not accept a higher price, which is the case for elastic demand.", "Quantity cannot fall when demand is perfectly inelastic, and the whole tax passes to the price.", "Revenue is £3 × 200 = £600, because the quantity sold is unchanged. It is not zero."]}
{"id": "econ-013", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.2", "lv": 1, "q": "A paper mill's production pollutes a river, harming a fishing club. The mill pays its own costs of £50 per tonne. The club's loss is £12 per tonne. What is the social cost per tonne?", "o": ["£62", "£50", "£38", "£12"], "a": 0, "x": "Social cost = private cost + external cost = £50 + £12 = £62. The trap is using only one part, or subtracting the external cost as if it were a benefit.", "w": ["", "£50 is only the private cost; it ignores the cost to third parties.", "Subtracting the external cost is wrong; external costs are added to private costs.", "£12 is only the external cost; social cost also includes the mill's own private cost."]}
{"id": "econ-014", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement describes a positive externality of consumption?", "o": ["The good is produced at a lower cost than other goods", "The consumer's benefit is higher than the price paid for the good", "Marginal social cost is greater than marginal private cost", "Third parties gain a benefit that is not reflected in the consumer's demand"], "a": 3, "x": "A positive externality of consumption is a benefit to third parties, so MSB is above MPB. The consumer ignores it, so demand (MPB) understates social benefit.", "w": ["Low production cost is not an externality; externalities affect third parties.", "This describes consumer surplus, not an external benefit to third parties.", "This describes a negative externality of production, not a consumption benefit.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-015", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Demand (MPB) is $P=20-Q$ and MPC is $P=4+Q$. The external cost is a constant £4 per unit, so MSC is $P=8+Q$. What is the welfare loss at the free-market output? (Price in £.)", "o": ["£8", "£4", "£2", "£16"], "a": 1, "x": "Market: 20−Q=4+Q gives Q=8. Optimum: 20−Q=8+Q gives Q=6. At Q=8 the gap between MSC (16) and MSB (12) is 4. Triangle = ½×2×4 = £4.", "w": ["Using the full rectangle 2×4 forgets that welfare loss is a triangle, so halve it.", "", "£2 is the excess output (8−6), not the area of the loss.", "£16 is the MSC at output 8; it is not the welfare loss."]}
{"id": "econ-016", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A firm produces 500 units. At this output MPC is £10 per unit and MSC is £14 per unit. What is the total external cost at this output?", "o": ["£2,000", "£5,000", "£4", "£7,000"], "a": 0, "x": "External cost per unit = MSC − MPC = £4. Total = £4 × 500 = £2,000. The trap is multiplying a curve's height, rather than the gap between curves, by output.", "w": ["", "This is MPC × output, the firm's private cost only.", "£4 is the external cost per unit; the question asks for the total.", "This is MSC × output, which is the total social cost, not the external cost."]}
{"id": "econ-017", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.2", "lv": 2, "q": "MPB is $P=40-Q$ and MPC=MSC is $P=10+Q$. The external benefit is £6 per unit, so MSB is $P=46-Q$. What is the welfare gain from moving to the social optimum?", "o": ["£9", "£18", "£6", "£3"], "a": 0, "x": "Market: 40−Q=10+Q gives Q=15. Optimum: 46−Q=10+Q gives Q=18. At Q=15 the MSB–MSC gap is 31−25=6. Gain = ½×3×6 = £9.", "w": ["", "£18 is 3×6, a rectangle; the gain is a triangle so you halve it.", "£6 is the external benefit per unit, not the area of the welfare gain.", "£3 is the extra output (18−15), not the area."]}
{"id": "econ-018", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Noisy fireworks impose costs on neighbours who do not buy them. Treating this as a negative externality of consumption, which statement is correct?", "o": ["MSC lies above MPC, so the free market over-produces the good", "MSB lies below MPB, so the free market under-consumes the good", "MSB lies above MPB, so the free market under-consumes the good", "MSB lies below MPB, so the free market over-consumes the good"], "a": 3, "x": "Harm to others in consumption makes MSB lie below MPB. Buyers equate MPB with MPC, so consumption exceeds the social optimum, where MSB=MSC, causing a welfare loss.", "w": ["This describes a negative externality of production; here the harm arises from consumption.", "MSB below MPB is correct, but it leads to over-consumption, not under-consumption.", "MSB above MPB is a positive consumption externality, giving under-consumption.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-037", "s": "micro", "t": "3.3.4", "lv": 1, "q": "At which output does a firm that wants to maximise profit produce?", "o": ["Where marginal revenue is at its highest", "Where average cost is at its lowest", "Where marginal cost equals marginal revenue, with MC cutting MR from below", "Where average revenue equals average cost"], "a": 2, "x": "Profit is maximised where MC = MR. Before that point, extra units add more to revenue than to cost. After it, they add more to cost. AR = AC is only normal profit, and minimum AC is not the rule.", "w": ["Highest MR is not the rule; MR must be compared with MC. Ignores the cost side.", "Minimum AC is productive efficiency, not profit maximisation. Mixes up cost minimising with profit maximising.", "", "AR = AC gives normal profit only; it is not the profit-maximising rule. Confuses breaking even with maximising profit."]}
{"id": "econ-038", "s": "micro", "t": "3.3.4", "lv": 1, "q": "A firm is earning normal profit. Which statement must be true?", "o": ["MR is greater than MC at the current output", "Total revenue is greater than total cost", "Average revenue equals average cost, covering the owner's opportunity cost", "Total revenue just covers total variable cost"], "a": 2, "x": "Normal profit is the minimum profit needed to keep the firm in its current use. AR = AC, so TR = TC, and costs include the owner's opportunity cost. TR > TC would be supernormal profit.", "w": ["At the profit-maximising output MR = MC, not MR > MC. Also says nothing about whether profit is normal.", "TR > TC means supernormal profit, not normal profit. Confuses normal with supernormal.", "", "TR = TVC is the short-run shutdown point, not normal profit. Ignores fixed costs."]}
{"id": "econ-039", "s": "micro", "t": "3.3.4", "lv": 2, "q": "A firm sells 200 units at a price of £12. At this output its average cost is £9 per unit. What is its supernormal profit?", "o": ["£1,800", "£2,400", "£3", "£600"], "a": 3, "x": "Supernormal profit = (AR − AC) × Q = (£12 − £9) × 200 = £600. The trap is giving profit per unit, or using total revenue or total cost alone.", "w": ["£1,800 is total cost (£9 × 200), not profit. Forgot to subtract it from revenue.", "£2,400 is total revenue (£12 × 200), not profit. Forgot to subtract total cost.", "£3 is profit per unit, not the total. Forgot to multiply by quantity.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-040", "s": "micro", "t": "3.3.4", "lv": 2, "q": "At 500 units a firm has AR £6, AVC £5 and AC £8. What should it do in the short run?", "o": ["Continue; it makes a supernormal profit as AR exceeds AVC", "Continue; its £1,000 loss is smaller than the £1,500 fixed costs lost on closing", "Shut down, because AR is below AC so it makes a loss", "Shut down, because that avoids the £1,000 loss from producing"], "a": 1, "x": "Loss = (£8 − £6) × 500 = £1,000. Fixed costs = (£8 − £5) × 500 = £1,500, paid even if it closes. AR exceeds AVC, so it continues. Compare AR with AVC, not AC.", "w": ["AR above AVC does not mean profit; AR is below AC, so there is a loss.", "", "Compares AR with AC. In the short run the test is AR against AVC, because fixed costs are unavoidable.", "Closing does not avoid the loss; it leaves a bigger loss of £1,500 in fixed costs."]}
{"id": "econ-041", "s": "micro", "t": "3.3.4", "lv": 2, "q": "A firm's MC is rising. At its current output of 40 units, MR is £15 and MC is £11. To raise profit, what should it do?", "o": ["Increase output until MR equals AC", "Cut output, because MR is greater than MC", "Keep output the same, because MR above MC means profit is maximised", "Increase output, because the extra unit adds more to revenue than to cost"], "a": 3, "x": "When MR > MC, each extra unit adds more revenue than cost, so profit rises. Output should expand until MC rises to equal MR. The profit-maximising condition is MC = MR, not MR = AC.", "w": ["The comparison is with MC, not AC. Using average cost instead of marginal cost is the slip.", "Cutting output is right only when MC exceeds MR. Reverses the rule.", "Profit is maximised only where MR = MC. At MR > MC there is still profit to gain.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-042", "s": "micro", "t": "3.3.4", "lv": 3, "q": "Output 1–5 units: TR £30, £56, £78, £96, £110; TC £20, £38, £57, £80, £105. At which output is profit maximised, and what kind of profit is it?", "o": ["3 units; supernormal profit of £21", "3 units; normal profit only, because MR exceeds MC", "2 units; supernormal profit of £18, where MC is lowest", "4 units; supernormal profit of £16, the first unit where MC exceeds MR"], "a": 0, "x": "MR: 30, 26, 22, 18, 14. MC: 20, 18, 19, 23, 25. At 3 units MR £22 > MC £19, and at 4 units MC £23 > MR £18. Profit at 3 units = £78 − £57 = £21, so supernormal.", "w": ["", "TR > TC gives supernormal profit. MR > MC only means the unit is worth producing, not that profit is normal.", "Lowest MC is not the rule. At 2 units MR (£26) still exceeds MC (£18), so profit can rise.", "At 4 units MC exceeds MR, so the fourth unit reduces profit from £21 to £16."]}
{"id": "econ-043", "s": "micro", "t": "3.4.4", "lv": 1, "q": "In a market, five groups hold these shares: A 30%, B 22%, C 18%, D 10%, all other firms combined 20%. What is the three-firm concentration ratio?", "o": ["70%", "80%", "20%", "30%"], "a": 0, "x": "Add the three largest shares: 30 + 22 + 18 = 70%. The trap is adding four firms (80%) or confusing the ratio with the leader's share or the 'others' share.", "w": ["", "80% adds the top four firms, which is the four-firm ratio, not the three-firm ratio.", "20% is the share of all other firms combined, not the combined share of the top three.", "30% is only the largest firm's share, so it is a one-firm ratio."]}
{"id": "econ-044", "s": "micro", "t": "3.4.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement best explains why firms in an oligopoly are described as interdependent?", "o": ["Each firm sells an identical product and must accept the market price", "Each firm is so small that its decisions have no effect on others", "Each firm can enter or leave the market at no cost, so rivals react", "Each firm's best price or output depends on how its few rivals are likely to react"], "a": 3, "x": "With only a few large firms, each one's decision affects rivals, who respond. Firms must therefore predict reactions. Price taking and tiny size describe perfect competition, not oligopoly.", "w": ["Identical products and price taking describe perfect competition, not interdependence between a few large firms.", "Tiny firms with no effect on others describe perfect competition; oligopolists are large enough to affect rivals.", "Costless entry and exit describe contestable or perfectly competitive markets; oligopoly has high barriers to entry.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-045", "s": "micro", "t": "3.4.4", "lv": 2, "q": "Two firms each choose High or Low price. Profits (£m, A then B): both High 10, 10; A High, B Low 4, 12; A Low, B High 12, 4; both Low 6, 6. They cannot collude. What is the likely outcome?", "o": ["Both Low, each earning £6m", "A Low and B High, with profits £12m and £4m", "A High and B Low, with profits £4m and £12m", "Both High, each earning £10m"], "a": 0, "x": "Low is each firm's dominant strategy: if the rival charges High, Low earns 12 not 10; if the rival charges Low, Low earns 6 not 4. So both choose Low, earning £6m. This is the prisoners' dilemma.", "w": ["", "B High is never best for B, since Low is dominant for B whatever A does.", "A High is never best for A, since Low is dominant for A whatever B does.", "Both High gives the best joint profit but needs collusion; without it each firm gains by switching to Low."]}
{"id": "econ-046", "s": "micro", "t": "3.4.4", "lv": 2, "q": "Three firms in a cartel agree to restrict output to keep the price high. Why is such an agreement likely to break down?", "o": ["Each member can raise its own profit by secretly producing more than its quota", "Restricting output always makes the firms' costs rise", "Cartels are only profitable if there are many firms in the market", "Higher prices reduce the barriers to entry so rivals leave"], "a": 0, "x": "At the high agreed price, each firm gains by cheating and selling extra. If all cheat, output rises and price falls. Cheating incentives, not barriers or costs, make collusion unstable.", "w": ["", "Restricting output does not always raise costs; the instability comes from the incentive to cheat, not from costs.", "Collusion is easier with few firms, which is why it is linked to oligopoly rather than many-firm markets.", "Higher profits tend to attract entry, but that is not why members cheat; this also reverses the role of barriers."]}
{"id": "econ-047", "s": "micro", "t": "3.4.4", "lv": 2, "q": "Total market sales are £250m. The four largest firms sell £60m, £50m, £30m and £20m. What is the three-firm concentration ratio?", "o": ["64%", "47%", "70%", "56%"], "a": 3, "x": "Top three sales = 60 + 50 + 30 = £140m. Divide by the market total: 140 ÷ 250 × 100 = 56%. Using four firms gives 64%, and averaging the three gives the wrong measure.", "w": ["64% is the four-firm ratio (160 ÷ 250), which includes the fourth firm.", "47% comes from dividing £140m by 3 (an average), which is not a share of the market.", "70% divides by £200m, which is not the total market sales given.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-048", "s": "micro", "t": "3.4.4", "lv": 3, "q": "A dominant supermarket in a four-firm oligopoly cuts prices to gain share. Which reason best explains why it may then prefer non-price competition?", "o": ["Rivals can match price cuts quickly, so shares stay similar and margins fall for all", "Non-price competition has no cost, unlike price cuts", "Demand is perfectly inelastic, so price cuts raise no revenue at all", "Price cuts are illegal in oligopoly markets"], "a": 0, "x": "Interdependence means rivals notice and match price cuts, so no share is gained and a price war lowers profits. Branding, quality or loyalty schemes are harder to copy quickly. Price cuts are not illegal.", "w": ["", "Advertising, quality and loyalty schemes cost money, so claiming non-price competition is costless is wrong.", "Demand is not perfectly inelastic; the problem is rivals matching the cut, not zero responsiveness.", "Price cuts are not generally illegal; only price-fixing agreements are, so this misstates the reason."]}
{"id": "econ-061", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.1", "lv": 1, "q": "What does the assumption of ceteris paribus mean when an economist analyses a change in the price of a good?", "o": ["The market is always in equilibrium", "Consumers always act irrationally", "All other factors affecting demand are held constant", "The price of every other good rises by the same percentage"], "a": 2, "x": "Ceteris paribus means 'all else equal'. Only the variable being studied changes, so its effect can be isolated. The trap is thinking it describes reality rather than being a simplifying assumption.", "w": ["Equilibrium is a separate concept; ceteris paribus is about holding other variables fixed.", "Irrationality has nothing to do with it; models often assume rational behaviour instead.", "", "Confuses ceteris paribus with a general price rise; it means other factors are held constant, not changed."]}
{"id": "econ-062", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Why is economics described as a social science?", "o": ["It studies human behaviour using scientific methods such as models and testing against evidence", "It is concerned only with the behaviour of firms", "It can prove laws with controlled laboratory experiments", "It only studies society's views, not data"], "a": 0, "x": "Economics applies scientific methods (building models, testing hypotheses against data) to human behaviour in society. The trap is claiming it can use controlled experiments like natural sciences.", "w": ["", "Wrong: economics also covers households, governments and whole economies, not just firms.", "Wrong: economies cannot be placed in a laboratory, which is a key difference from natural sciences.", "Wrong: economists use data and evidence to test theories, not just opinions."]}
{"id": "econ-063", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which is the main reason economists cannot test a theory about a whole economy by a controlled experiment?", "o": ["Economists lack any data", "Governments forbid all research", "Economic theories are always correct", "Other factors cannot be held constant across a real economy"], "a": 3, "x": "In a real economy many variables change at once and cannot be controlled, so the effect of one change is hard to isolate. This is why economists rely on models and ceteris paribus.", "w": ["Data exists in large amounts; the problem is isolating one variable, not lack of data.", "Research is not generally forbidden; the issue is practical control of variables.", "Theories are tested and often revised, so they are not always correct.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-064", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.1", "lv": 2, "q": "A model of the market for coffee predicts that a rise in income raises demand. Real data show demand fell after incomes rose, in a year when a new cheaper rival drink launched. What best explains this?", "o": ["Income has no effect on demand", "The model is proved wrong with certainty", "Ceteris paribus did not hold, as the price of a substitute changed", "Coffee must be an inferior good"], "a": 2, "x": "The prediction assumed other factors constant. A cheaper substitute shifted demand the other way, so ceteris paribus failed. The trap is rejecting the model outright or labelling the good inferior without isolating the effect.", "w": ["Income can affect demand; the other change is a better explanation.", "One observation cannot prove a model wrong when other factors changed at the same time.", "", "Concluding inferior good ignores the substitute launch, which is a confounding change."]}
{"id": "econ-065", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.1", "lv": 2, "q": "A model assumes consumers are rational and have perfect information. Which is the most valid evaluation of this model?", "o": ["The assumptions make the model useless", "Assumptions matter only in natural sciences", "Assumptions mean the model is always accurate", "Simplifying makes analysis manageable, but predictions may be less accurate if real behaviour differs"], "a": 3, "x": "Assumptions simplify reality so that cause and effect can be analysed, but where they are unrealistic, predictions may be weaker. Good evaluation weighs both, not just one side.", "w": ["Too extreme: simplification is a strength as well as a limitation, so models are still useful.", "Wrong: all models, including in economics, rely on assumptions.", "Wrong: unrealistic assumptions can reduce accuracy, so accuracy is not guaranteed.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-066", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.1", "lv": 3, "q": "A toy shop's owner says: 'Cutting my prices by 10% will raise sales, ceteris paribus.' Over the next month sales fall because a rival opens next door. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["The claim is disproved because sales fell", "The claim was wrong because ceteris paribus cannot be used by businesses", "The claim is not disproved, as the rival's entry broke the ceteris paribus condition", "The claim is proved because prices were cut"], "a": 2, "x": "The prediction held only if other factors stayed constant. The rival's entry changed demand, so the result does not test the claim fairly. Observed outcomes mix several changes, which is why controlled experiments are impossible.", "w": ["Falling sales alone do not disprove it, as another factor changed at the same time.", "Ceteris paribus is used by all economic decision makers, including businesses.", "", "Cutting prices cannot prove anything when other factors changed; the result is inconclusive."]}
{"id": "econ-067", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is a positive statement?", "o": ["The government should raise the minimum wage to help low-paid workers", "Unemployment in the UK is too high", "Healthcare is more important than defence spending", "A rise in income tax will reduce the incomes of households"], "a": 3, "x": "A rise in income tax reducing household incomes can be tested against evidence. The others use value words (should, too high, more important). The trap is thinking only true statements are positive.", "w": ["'Should' signals a value judgement, so it is normative, not testable by data alone.", "'Too high' depends on opinion about what level is acceptable, so it is normative.", "'More important' ranks priorities by opinion, so it is normative.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-068", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement is normative?", "o": ["The unemployment rate fell between two quarters", "Wealthy households ought to pay a higher rate of tax", "UK inflation rose in 2022 compared with 2020", "Raising fuel duty will reduce demand for petrol"], "a": 1, "x": "'Ought to' expresses a value judgement about fairness. It cannot be proved or disproved with data. The other three can be checked against evidence, so they are positive.", "w": ["This reports a measurable change, so it is positive.", "", "This is a claim about data that can be checked, so it is positive.", "Predicts an effect that can be tested with evidence, so it is positive even if the prediction might be wrong."]}
{"id": "econ-069", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.2", "lv": 2, "q": "'The UK economy grew by 8% last year, so the government is clearly doing a good job.' Which description of this statement is correct?", "o": ["Neither, because it is a political statement", "A positive claim about growth combined with a normative judgement", "Entirely positive, because it contains a statistic", "Entirely normative, because it mentions the government"], "a": 1, "x": "The growth claim is testable, but 'doing a good job' is a value judgement. Statements can mix both. The trap is labelling a whole sentence positive because it includes a number.", "w": ["Economic statements about government can be classified; this one has both parts.", "", "Including a number does not make the whole statement positive; 'a good job' is opinion.", "Mentioning the government does not make it entirely normative; the growth figure can be tested."]}
{"id": "econ-070", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A student says: 'The statement that a minimum wage increases unemployment is normative, because the evidence is disputed.' What is the error?", "o": ["It is positive, since it can in principle be tested, even if evidence is disputed", "It is normative because it concerns workers' welfare", "It is neither, since disputed claims cannot be classified", "It is normative because economists disagree"], "a": 0, "x": "A statement is positive if it can be tested against evidence, even if the evidence is mixed or it turns out false. Disagreement about the evidence does not make it a value judgement.", "w": ["", "Topic being welfare does not make a claim normative; the claim is about cause and effect.", "Disputed claims are still classified by whether they can be tested, so it is positive.", "Disagreement over evidence is not the test; testability is. This confuses 'disputed' with 'opinion'."]}
{"id": "econ-071", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Why do value judgements matter when a government chooses between two policies that economists agree have different effects?", "o": ["They make normative statements testable", "Positive analysis alone cannot say which outcome is preferable, such as equity versus efficiency", "Value judgements determine which facts are true", "Value judgements replace the need for any evidence"], "a": 1, "x": "Positive economics can show the effects of a policy, but choosing between outcomes, such as greater equity or greater efficiency, needs value judgements. Policy therefore combines evidence with opinion.", "w": ["Normative statements stay untestable by data alone, whatever the policy context.", "", "Values do not change what is true; facts remain testable regardless of opinion.", "Good policy still uses evidence; values guide the choice, they do not replace it."]}
{"id": "econ-072", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Which statement could be tested using data, even if it might turn out to be false?", "o": ["Sugary drinks ought to be taxed more heavily", "The tax on sugary drinks is unfair to poorer households", "Reducing sugar consumption is more valuable than lower prices", "A tax on sugary drinks will reduce consumption of sugary drinks"], "a": 3, "x": "Whether the tax reduces consumption can be checked with sales data, so it is positive whether true or false. The other statements rely on fairness or priorities, which are value judgements.", "w": ["'Ought' is a value judgement, so it is normative.", "'Unfair' is a value judgement about fairness, so it is normative.", "'More valuable' ranks outcomes by opinion, so it is normative.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-073", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement best defines scarcity in economics?", "o": ["A shortage of a good at the current market price", "Goods that are expensive to buy", "Resources that are running out and cannot be replaced", "Finite resources set against unlimited wants"], "a": 3, "x": "Scarcity is the basic economic problem: finite resources cannot satisfy unlimited wants. It exists for every economy, rich or poor. Confusing it with a market shortage is the common slip.", "w": ["A shortage is excess demand at one price; scarcity is a permanent, universal problem, so this confuses the two.", "Price is not the issue; scarcity exists even for cheap or free goods.", "This describes non-renewable resources only; scarcity applies to all resources, including renewable ones.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-074", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which of the following is a renewable resource?", "o": ["Timber from a managed forest", "Iron ore", "Natural gas", "Coal"], "a": 0, "x": "Renewable resources can regenerate if managed sustainably, such as managed forests. Coal, gas and iron ore are finite stocks that cannot be replaced on a human timescale. The trap is thinking renewable means unlimited.", "w": ["", "Iron ore is a mineral deposit that is used up once extracted, so it is non-renewable.", "Natural gas takes millions of years to form, so it is non-renewable.", "Coal is a fossil fuel formed over millions of years, so it is non-renewable."]}
{"id": "econ-075", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.3", "lv": 2, "q": "A student has a free Saturday. Options ranked: 1) paid shift earning £60, 2) revising, 3) football with friends. She chooses the shift. What is her opportunity cost?", "o": ["£60", "Revising", "Football with friends", "Revising and football together"], "a": 1, "x": "Opportunity cost is the next best alternative forgone. Revising is ranked second, so it is the cost. Adding all alternatives together is the common error, as is quoting the £60 gained.", "w": ["£60 is the benefit of the chosen option, not what is given up.", "", "Football is her third choice, not the next best alternative.", "Opportunity cost counts only the single next best alternative, not all forgone options combined."]}
{"id": "econ-076", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.3", "lv": 2, "q": "A farmer uses a field to grow wheat worth £8,000 a year. The next best use is rapeseed worth £6,500, and the third is grazing worth £5,000. What is the opportunity cost of growing wheat?", "o": ["£6,500 of rapeseed", "£5,000 of grazing", "£1,500, the difference between wheat and rapeseed", "£11,500 from rapeseed and grazing"], "a": 0, "x": "The opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative: rapeseed, £6,500. Adding the other options or subtracting from the wheat income are the usual slips.", "w": ["", "Grazing is the third best use, not the next best.", "Subtracting gives a net gain, not the value of the forgone option.", "Adding all alternatives is wrong; only the next best is the opportunity cost."]}
{"id": "econ-077", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.3", "lv": 2, "q": "The government spends £2 billion on a new hospital rather than on new railway upgrades, its next best use of funds. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["There is no opportunity cost, as the government can borrow", "The opportunity cost is the value of all other public projects", "The opportunity cost is the hospital's £2 billion", "The opportunity cost is the benefit of the railway upgrades"], "a": 3, "x": "The opportunity cost is the benefit forgone from the next best use: the railway upgrades. Borrowing does not remove it, since resources used for the hospital cannot be used elsewhere.", "w": ["Borrowing still uses real resources and leaves other projects unfunded, so an opportunity cost remains.", "Only the next best alternative counts, not every other project.", "£2 billion is the money cost of the hospital, not the forgone alternative.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-078", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.3", "lv": 3, "q": "A council can use a site for a £4m car park, a £5m leisure centre or £3m of housing. It builds the leisure centre. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["Opportunity cost is £3m, as housing is the least valuable", "Opportunity cost is £7m, from car park and housing combined", "Opportunity cost is £4m, the value of the car park", "Opportunity cost is £5m, the cost of the centre"], "a": 2, "x": "Forgone options rank car park £4m then housing £3m. The next best alternative to the leisure centre is the car park, so £4m. Adding alternatives or picking the lowest are common errors.", "w": ["The least valuable option is not the next best; the car park is worth more.", "Only the next best alternative counts; adding both overstates it.", "", "£5m is the value of the chosen option, not the forgone one."]}
{"id": "econ-079", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.4", "lv": 1, "q": "A point inside a country's PPF shows that the economy is:", "o": ["Using resources inefficiently or leaving some unemployed", "Using resources fully and efficiently", "Producing an unattainable combination of goods", "Experiencing economic growth"], "a": 0, "x": "Points inside the PPF mean resources are unemployed or misallocated, so output is below potential. Points on the curve are efficient; points outside are unattainable with current resources.", "w": ["", "Full and efficient use puts the economy on the PPF, not inside it.", "Unattainable combinations lie outside the PPF, not inside.", "Growth is an outward shift of the PPF, not a point inside it."]}
{"id": "econ-080", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Which event would shift a country's PPF outwards?", "o": ["A fall in unemployment", "A move from one point on the PPF to another", "A recession ending", "A rise in the quantity and quality of capital goods"], "a": 3, "x": "Growth in productive capacity, such as more or better capital, shifts the PPF outwards. Falling unemployment or ending a recession only moves the economy towards the existing curve.", "w": ["Lower unemployment moves the economy from inside towards the curve; it does not shift the curve.", "Moving along the curve is a trade-off, not growth.", "Ending a recession moves output towards the PPF, not the PPF itself.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-081", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.4", "lv": 2, "q": "An economy on its PPF can produce 100 units of consumer goods and 0 capital goods, or 60 consumer and 20 capital goods. Moving between these points, the opportunity cost of one capital good is:", "o": ["0.5 consumer goods", "3 consumer goods", "40 consumer goods", "2 consumer goods"], "a": 3, "x": "Capital rises by 20 and consumer goods fall by 40. Opportunity cost per capital good is 40 ÷ 20 = 2 consumer goods. Dividing the wrong way round gives the cost of a consumer good.", "w": ["20 ÷ 40 is the opportunity cost of a consumer good in capital goods, inverted.", "3 uses 60 ÷ 20, but 60 is the final consumer output, not the amount given up.", "40 is the total sacrificed, not the cost per unit of capital.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-082", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.4", "lv": 2, "q": "A straight-line PPF between consumer goods and capital goods implies that:", "o": ["Resources are perfectly specialised", "The economy is inefficient", "Opportunity cost is constant", "Opportunity cost is increasing"], "a": 2, "x": "A straight-line PPF has a constant slope, so the opportunity cost is constant. This means resources are equally suited to both goods. A bowed-out PPF shows increasing opportunity cost.", "w": ["Specialised resources give a bowed curve; a straight line means they transfer equally well.", "Points on a PPF are efficient whatever its shape.", "", "Increasing opportunity cost gives a PPF that is concave (bowed outwards), not straight."]}
{"id": "econ-083", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.4", "lv": 2, "q": "A government diverts resources from consumer goods to capital goods. Other things equal, the likely long-run effect on the PPF is:", "o": ["The PPF shifts inwards because consumption falls", "The economy moves to a point outside the current PPF immediately", "The PPF shifts outwards as productive capacity grows", "There is no effect because the economy stays on the same curve"], "a": 2, "x": "More capital goods raise productive capacity, so the PPF shifts outwards over time. The sacrifice of consumption now is the opportunity cost. Output cannot exceed the current PPF immediately.", "w": ["Falling current consumption is a movement along the curve, not a loss of capacity.", "Points outside the current PPF are unattainable now; growth takes time.", "", "Investment in capital increases capacity, so the curve moves rather than staying fixed."]}
{"id": "econ-084", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.4", "lv": 3, "q": "A technological advance doubles the output per worker in capital goods only. The maximum consumer goods output is unchanged. The PPF (capital on the vertical axis) will:", "o": ["Shift out parallel on both axes", "Shift out on the consumer axis only", "Shift out on the capital axis only, pivoting at the consumer axis intercept", "Shift inwards on the capital axis"], "a": 2, "x": "Only the capital maximum rises, so the vertical intercept moves up while the consumer intercept stays fixed. The curve pivots outwards. A parallel shift needs both goods' capacity to rise.", "w": ["A parallel shift needs capacity to rise for both goods; here only capital improves.", "The horizontal intercept is unchanged because consumer goods technology has not changed.", "", "Better technology raises capacity; it cannot lower the maximum."]}
{"id": "econ-085", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.5", "lv": 1, "q": "Which is the best definition of the division of labour?", "o": ["Sharing total output equally between all workers", "Workers choosing between work and leisure", "Splitting production into separate tasks, each done by a different worker", "Each country producing only goods in which it has a cost advantage"], "a": 2, "x": "Division of labour splits production into separate tasks, each done by different workers. Specialisation is the wider term for focusing on one activity; confusing it with country-level trade or income sharing is the slip.", "w": ["This describes distribution of income, not how production is organised.", "This is the work-leisure choice, not a production method.", "", "This describes international specialisation by comparative advantage, not division of labour within production."]}
{"id": "econ-086", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.5", "lv": 1, "q": "Which is a likely DISADVANTAGE of the division of labour for a worker on a bakery production line?", "o": ["Greater skill from repeating one task", "Higher output per hour from each worker", "Boredom from repetitive tasks, lowering motivation", "Time saved by not switching between tasks"], "a": 2, "x": "Repetitive tasks cause boredom and lower motivation, a key drawback. The other options are benefits: higher productivity, no switching time and greater dexterity.", "w": ["Skill from repetition is a benefit of specialisation.", "This is a benefit of specialising, not a drawback.", "", "Saving switching time is a benefit; the slip is listing advantages as drawbacks."]}
{"id": "econ-087", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.5", "lv": 2, "q": "In a barter economy, Asha makes bread and wants shoes, but the shoemaker wants eggs, not bread. Which problem does this show, and which function of money solves it?", "o": ["Lack of a unit of account; store of value", "Diminishing returns; medium of exchange", "Opportunity cost; standard of deferred payment", "Double coincidence of wants; medium of exchange"], "a": 3, "x": "Barter needs a double coincidence of wants: each party must want what the other offers. Money as a medium of exchange removes this, since everyone accepts it. Unit of account only gives prices.", "w": ["Unit of account helps compare values but does not remove the need for matching wants.", "Diminishing returns concern production, not exchange.", "Opportunity cost is not the barter problem; deferred payment relates to future debts.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-088", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.5", "lv": 2, "q": "A firm splits making a chair into 4 tasks. Before, 5 workers each made 2 chairs a day (10 in total). After, the same 5 workers make 18 chairs a day. What is the output per worker now?", "o": ["4 chairs", "8 chairs", "3.6 chairs", "1.8 chairs"], "a": 2, "x": "Labour productivity is output divided by workers: 18 ÷ 5 = 3.6 chairs per worker, up from 2. Using the wrong divisor, such as the number of tasks, gives the wrong answer.", "w": ["Dividing 18 by 4.5 is not valid; the divisor must be the 5 workers, not the tasks or a guess.", "8 is the increase in total output (18 − 10), not output per worker.", "", "1.8 comes from dividing 18 by 10, the old total output, not by workers."]}
{"id": "econ-089", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.5", "lv": 2, "q": "Why does specialisation lead to trade?", "o": ["Workers produce more than they need and exchange the surplus for other goods", "Government requires firms to sell outputs abroad", "Money is eliminated, so goods must be bartered", "Workers produce less than they need and must import the rest"], "a": 0, "x": "Specialists produce a surplus beyond their own needs, and they need other goods they no longer make, so they exchange. Trade follows from specialisation, not from government orders or the end of money.", "w": ["", "Trade arises from interdependence, not legal compulsion.", "Specialisation increases the need for money as a medium of exchange, not barter.", "Specialisation raises output; the issue is a surplus of one good, not a shortfall of everything."]}
{"id": "econ-090", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.5", "lv": 3, "q": "A town's economy relies heavily on one car factory using extreme division of labour. The factory closes. Which drawback of specialisation does this best illustrate?", "o": ["A fall in labour productivity due to boredom", "Loss of the medium of exchange function of money", "Over-dependence on one industry, with workers' narrow skills hard to reuse", "Diminishing marginal utility for consumers"], "a": 2, "x": "Specialisation creates over-dependence and occupational immobility: narrowly skilled workers struggle to find other jobs when the industry fails. Boredom is a different drawback, and money functions are unaffected.", "w": ["Boredom is a drawback in work, but a closure is about dependence, not falling productivity.", "Money still works as a medium of exchange; this confuses specialisation with money's functions.", "", "Diminishing marginal utility concerns consumption, not job losses."]}
{"id": "econ-091", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.6", "lv": 1, "q": "In a free market economy, which mechanism mainly allocates resources?", "o": ["Government production targets", "The price mechanism", "Rationing by the state", "Central planning committees"], "a": 1, "x": "In a free market, prices signal scarcity and consumer demand, so resources are allocated through the price mechanism. Government targets and central planning belong to command economies.", "w": ["Targets are a feature of command economies, not free markets.", "", "Rationing is state allocation, typical of command systems.", "Central planning is the command economy method, not the free market one."]}
{"id": "econ-092", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.6", "lv": 1, "q": "Which economist is most associated with the idea of the 'invisible hand'?", "o": ["John Maynard Keynes", "Adam Smith", "Friedrich Hayek", "Karl Marx"], "a": 1, "x": "Adam Smith argued that individuals pursuing self-interest in markets unintentionally promote the wider good, as if guided by an invisible hand. Marx and Hayek are linked to other ideas.", "w": ["Keynes is linked to government demand management, not this idea.", "", "Hayek stressed dispersed knowledge and prices, not the phrase 'invisible hand'.", "Marx criticised capitalism and favoured collective ownership of the means of production."]}
{"id": "econ-093", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.6", "lv": 2, "q": "Hayek argued against central planning mainly because planners:", "o": ["Ignore the profits of private owners", "Lack the dispersed knowledge that prices communicate", "Always produce too many consumer goods", "Cannot collect enough tax revenue"], "a": 1, "x": "Hayek's point is knowledge: information about wants and costs is scattered among millions of people, and prices bring it together. Planners cannot gather it, so allocation is poor.", "w": ["Profit is not the core point; it is the knowledge problem.", "", "He did not claim planners overproduce consumer goods; the issue is poor information.", "Hayek's argument concerns information, not the government's tax revenue."]}
{"id": "econ-094", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.6", "lv": 2, "q": "A government runs the NHS and state schools but leaves most goods to private firms. This economy is best described as:", "o": ["A command economy, because the state provides some services", "A subsistence economy, because some goods are free", "A free market economy, because most goods are private", "A mixed economy, because both state and market allocate resources"], "a": 3, "x": "A mixed economy combines market allocation with significant state provision and regulation. Some state provision does not make an economy a command one; that needs the state to dominate allocation.", "w": ["Command economies have state control of most resources, not just some services.", "Subsistence means producing mainly for own needs, which is not described.", "Free markets have minimal state provision; here the state provides major services.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-095", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.6", "lv": 2, "q": "Which is a typical disadvantage of a command economy compared with a free market?", "o": ["Prices change too quickly", "Inequality is always greater", "Weak incentives and little consumer choice", "Unemployment is always higher"], "a": 2, "x": "Without profit incentives and market signals, command economies often suffer inefficiency, shortages or surpluses, and limited choice. Claims using 'always' about unemployment or inequality are wrong.", "w": ["Prices are often fixed in command economies, so this is not a typical weakness.", "Command systems aim for lower inequality, so this is the reverse.", "", "Command economies typically claim full employment; 'always higher' is unsupported."]}
{"id": "econ-096", "s": "micro", "t": "1.1.6", "lv": 3, "q": "Marx believed capitalism would be replaced by collective ownership. Which statement best fits his view of the free market?", "o": ["It allocates resources efficiently without intervention", "It works best when the state sets all prices", "It exploits workers because owners of capital take surplus value", "It cannot produce any economic growth"], "a": 2, "x": "Marx argued capital owners exploit workers by keeping surplus value, creating class conflict. He did not deny that capitalism produces growth. Efficient self-correction is Smith's view instead.", "w": ["This is closer to Smith's view, which Marx disputed.", "Price setting is not the core of Marx's critique; ownership and exploitation are.", "", "Marx accepted capitalism generated production and growth, while criticising its distribution and ownership."]}
{"id": "econ-097", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.1", "lv": 1, "q": "In standard economic theory, what do rational consumers aim to maximise when they decide what to buy?", "o": ["Their total spending", "The number of goods they consume", "The profit of the firms they buy from", "Their utility"], "a": 3, "x": "Rational consumers are assumed to maximise utility, the satisfaction gained from consuming goods and services. Confusing utility with quantity consumed is the trap: more goods do not always mean more satisfaction.", "w": ["Spending is not the aim; consumers want satisfaction from what they buy, not to spend as much as possible.", "Quantity is not the goal; beyond some point extra units add little or negative utility.", "Consumers do not aim at firms' profits; that is the firm's objective, not the consumer's.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-098", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which profit-maximising rule do firms follow under the standard assumption of rational behaviour?", "o": ["Produce where average revenue is highest", "Produce where total cost is lowest", "Produce where marginal revenue equals marginal cost", "Produce where marginal cost is at its minimum"], "a": 2, "x": "Profit is maximised where MR = MC, with the last unit adding as much to revenue as to cost. Minimising cost or maximising revenue alone does not guarantee maximum profit.", "w": ["Highest average revenue ignores costs, so profit need not be maximised.", "Lowest total cost would mean producing nothing; it ignores revenue entirely.", "", "Minimum MC is the lowest cost of a unit, not where marginal revenue matches it."]}
{"id": "econ-099", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.1", "lv": 2, "q": "A shop owner finds that selling the 51st unit of a product adds £8 to revenue and £11 to cost. If the owner is a rational profit maximiser, what should they do?", "o": ["Reduce output, as MC exceeds MR", "Keep output at 51, as revenue is still rising", "Increase output, as MR is positive", "Raise output until MC falls to zero"], "a": 0, "x": "MC (£11) is greater than MR (£8), so the 51st unit lowers profit by £3. A rational profit maximiser cuts output until MR = MC. The trap is that positive MR alone does not justify production.", "w": ["", "Rising revenue is irrelevant if cost rises by more; profit falls.", "Positive MR is not enough; the extra unit costs more than it earns.", "Output is not pushed until MC is zero; the rule compares MC with MR."]}
{"id": "econ-100", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.1", "lv": 2, "q": "A consumer buys a sandwich at £4 and gets satisfaction they value at £6. Which statement best fits the assumption of rational behaviour?", "o": ["Buying is rational because the benefit exceeds the cost", "Buying is irrational because the consumer gains less than £4", "Buying is irrational because all goods should be valued equally", "Buying is rational only if the sandwich is the cheapest available"], "a": 0, "x": "A rational consumer buys when perceived benefit (£6) exceeds cost (£4), giving a net gain of £2. Rationality is about weighing costs and benefits, not choosing the cheapest option.", "w": ["", "The gain is £6 against £4 cost, so there is a net benefit, not a loss.", "Goods are valued differently by individuals; rationality does not require equal valuation.", "Rational choice weighs benefit against cost; it need not be the cheapest option."]}
{"id": "econ-101", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.1", "lv": 2, "q": "A shopper buys a product because a sign says 'Only 2 left!' even though they did not plan to. Which comment is most accurate?", "o": ["It shows the firm is maximising social welfare", "It shows demand is perfectly price inelastic", "It suggests behaviour may deviate from rational utility maximisation", "It proves consumers are always rational"], "a": 2, "x": "Responding to scarcity messages rather than weighing costs and benefits is an example of behavioural influences that challenge the assumption of rationality. One example does not prove anything about elasticity.", "w": ["The sign is a firm's sales tactic; it says nothing about social welfare.", "Nothing here measures price responsiveness, so elasticity cannot be concluded.", "", "Impulse buying prompted by a sign cuts against the rationality assumption, not for it."]}
{"id": "econ-102", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.1", "lv": 3, "q": "Which is a limitation of assuming that firms always maximise profit?", "o": ["Profit maximisation is only possible in perfect competition", "Firms always know their exact marginal revenue and cost", "Owners may pursue other aims, such as sales growth or social goals", "Firms ignore costs when setting output"], "a": 2, "x": "Firms may have other objectives, such as revenue or sales maximisation, growth, or satisfying shareholders, and may lack full information. This weakens the assumption. Claiming perfect information would support it, not limit it.", "w": ["Firms in any market structure can aim at MR = MC, so this is false.", "Perfect knowledge would support the assumption; in practice firms lack it, which is the limitation.", "", "Profit maximisers consider costs; ignoring them is not the assumption or the limitation."]}
{"id": "econ-103", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.2", "lv": 1, "q": "The price of a coffee shop's latte falls from £3.20 to £2.80 and more lattes are bought. What does this show on the demand curve for lattes?", "o": ["An extension of demand, a movement down the curve", "A leftward shift of the demand curve", "A contraction of demand, a movement up the curve", "A rightward shift of the demand curve"], "a": 0, "x": "A fall in the good's own price causes an extension of demand: a movement along the existing curve. A shift needs a change in a condition of demand, not price itself.", "w": ["", "Price is on the axis, so it cannot shift the curve, and the direction would also be wrong.", "A contraction is caused by a price rise, not a fall; direction is wrong.", "Price changes do not shift the curve; confuses a movement along with a shift."]}
{"id": "econ-104", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which one of the following would cause the demand curve for train travel to shift to the right?", "o": ["A fall in consumers' real incomes, train travel being a normal good", "A rise in the price of petrol, a substitute", "A fall in train fares", "A rise in the price of train tickets"], "a": 1, "x": "Dearer petrol makes car travel, a substitute, less attractive, so more people want trains at every price: demand shifts right. Own-price changes only cause movements along the curve.", "w": ["Lower income reduces demand for a normal good, shifting the curve left.", "", "Own price falling causes an extension along the curve, not a shift.", "Own price rising causes a contraction along the curve, not a shift."]}
{"id": "econ-105", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Marginal utility is best defined as the:", "o": ["price a consumer pays for an extra unit of a good", "average satisfaction per unit consumed", "total satisfaction from consuming all units of a good", "satisfaction gained from consuming one extra unit of a good"], "a": 3, "x": "Marginal utility is the additional satisfaction from consuming one more unit. Do not confuse it with total utility, which is the sum of all the marginal utilities.", "w": ["Price is what is paid, not satisfaction gained; utility is not the same as price.", "That is average utility, which is not the marginal concept.", "That is total utility, not marginal utility.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-106", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.2", "lv": 2, "q": "Cinema tickets and streaming subscriptions are substitutes. The price of streaming subscriptions rises. What happens to the demand curve for cinema tickets, and why?", "o": ["It shifts left, as consumers switch away from cinemas", "It shifts right, as cinemas become relatively cheaper", "It shifts right, as cinema tickets become cheaper in absolute terms", "It stays still, with a contraction along the curve"], "a": 1, "x": "With substitutes, a rise in one price raises demand for the other at every price, so the curve shifts right. Cinema ticket prices have not changed, so there is no movement along the curve.", "w": ["That is the pattern for complements; mixes up substitutes and complements.", "", "Cinema prices have not fallen; only their relative price has changed.", "The cinema price has not changed, so there is no contraction; the related price shifts the curve."]}
{"id": "econ-107", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A consumer eats slices of pizza. Marginal utility, in utils, is 20, 14, 8, 2 for slices 1 to 4. What is total utility after three slices, and what does the pattern show?", "o": ["34 utils; diminishing marginal utility", "42 utils; diminishing marginal utility", "8 utils; diminishing marginal utility", "42 utils; total utility is falling"], "a": 1, "x": "Total utility is the sum of marginal utilities: 20 + 14 + 8 = 42. Marginal utility falls with each extra slice, which is diminishing marginal utility; total utility still rises while marginal utility is positive.", "w": ["34 is 20 + 14 only, the first two slices; the third slice was left out.", "", "8 is only the third slice's marginal utility, not the total; marginal and total confused.", "The sum is correct, but total utility does not fall here; marginal utility does."]}
{"id": "econ-108", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.2", "lv": 3, "q": "Pasta and pasta sauce are complements. A supermarket cuts the price of pasta. Which pair of changes to the demand curve for sauce, and to the quantity of pasta demanded, is correct?", "o": ["Sauce curve unchanged; pasta quantity extends", "Sauce curve shifts right; pasta quantity extends", "Sauce curve shifts left; pasta quantity contracts", "Sauce curve shifts right; pasta curve shifts right"], "a": 1, "x": "Cheaper pasta extends the quantity of pasta demanded along its own curve. As a complement, more pasta raises demand for sauce at every price, shifting its curve right. Pasta's own curve does not shift.", "w": ["Ignores the complement link: a related price change shifts the sauce curve.", "", "Treats complements as substitutes and gets the pasta direction wrong too.", "Pasta's own price change only moves along its curve; it does not shift it."]}
{"id": "econ-109", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.4", "lv": 1, "q": "The price of bread rises and bakeries produce more loaves. How is this shown on a supply diagram?", "o": ["A movement down along the supply curve", "A movement up along the supply curve", "A rightward shift of the supply curve", "A leftward shift of the supply curve"], "a": 1, "x": "A change in the good's own price causes a movement along the supply curve. A higher price gives an extension of supply. The trap is calling it a shift.", "w": ["A fall in price would cause a movement down, a contraction; here the price has risen.", "", "Shifts come from changes in conditions of supply, not from the good's own price changing.", "A leftward shift means supply falls at every price, which is not what happens here."]}
{"id": "econ-110", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Which one of the following would shift the supply curve for new cars to the right?", "o": ["A new technology that lowers the cost of making cars", "A rise in the price of new cars", "A rise in the price of steel used to make cars", "A fall in the price of new cars"], "a": 0, "x": "Lower costs of production through technology are a condition of supply, so supply increases at every price. Changes in the car's own price only move along the curve.", "w": ["", "The good's own price causes a movement along the curve, not a shift.", "Higher input costs raise costs of production and shift supply left.", "A fall in own price causes a contraction along the curve, not a shift."]}
{"id": "econ-111", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.4", "lv": 2, "q": "The government introduces a per-unit subsidy on the production of solar panels. What is the effect on the supply curve?", "o": ["It shifts left, as firms receive less", "It becomes steeper but does not shift", "It stays unchanged; only demand is affected", "It shifts right, as costs of production fall"], "a": 3, "x": "A subsidy lowers firms' costs per unit, so they supply more at every price. The supply curve shifts right (down). Subsidies are a condition of supply, not a demand factor.", "w": ["That describes an indirect tax, which raises costs and shifts supply left.", "A per-unit subsidy gives a parallel shift; steepness changes only for percentage-based taxes or subsidies.", "Subsidies are paid to producers, so they affect supply directly.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-112", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.4", "lv": 2, "q": "A poor harvest cuts wheat output. At the same time, the price of wheat rises. Which describes the supply diagram?", "o": ["Rightward shift, then an extension along the new curve", "Contraction along the original curve only", "Leftward shift of supply, then an extension along the new curve", "Leftward shift of supply, then a contraction along the new curve"], "a": 2, "x": "The harvest is a conditions-of-supply change, shifting supply left. The higher price then causes an extension along the new curve. Both effects need identifying separately.", "w": ["A poor harvest reduces supply, so the shift is left, not right.", "This ignores the shift caused by the harvest, which is a condition of supply.", "", "A price rise causes an extension, not a contraction."]}
{"id": "econ-113", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.4", "lv": 2, "q": "A toy shop's supplier raises wholesale prices by 10%. The shop buys the toys to resell. What is the effect on the shop's supply of toys?", "o": ["Extension along the supply curve", "Leftward shift of the supply curve", "Contraction along the supply curve", "Rightward shift of the supply curve"], "a": 1, "x": "Higher wholesale prices raise the shop's costs, so it supplies less at every price. The curve shifts left. Do not confuse input costs with the good's own price.", "w": ["Extension needs a rise in the toy's own selling price.", "", "Contraction needs a fall in the good's own price, not a rise in costs.", "A rightward shift needs lower costs or a subsidy."]}
{"id": "econ-114", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.4", "lv": 3, "q": "Supply of a good is $Q_s = 40 + 5P$ (P in £, Q in units). A new tax cuts supply by 20 units at every price. What is quantity supplied at £6 after the tax?", "o": ["70 units", "10 units", "50 units", "30 units"], "a": 2, "x": "Before the tax at £6: $40+5\\times6=70$. A 20-unit fall at every price gives 50. This is a leftward shift of the curve, not a movement along it.", "w": ["That is the original quantity at £6, ignoring the shift.", "That is an arithmetic slip; 70 minus 20 is 50, not 10.", "", "That subtracts 40 from the original quantity; the shift is only 20 units."]}
{"id": "econ-115", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.5", "lv": 1, "q": "Which formula gives price elasticity of supply (PES)?", "o": ["$\\frac{\\%\\Delta Q_d}{\\%\\Delta P}$", "$\\frac{\\Delta Q_s}{\\Delta P}$", "$\\frac{\\%\\Delta P}{\\%\\Delta Q_s}$", "$\\frac{\\%\\Delta Q_s}{\\%\\Delta P}$"], "a": 3, "x": "PES is the percentage change in quantity supplied divided by the percentage change in price. Inverting it or using absolute changes is the common slip.", "w": ["This is the formula for PED, which uses quantity demanded.", "This uses absolute changes, which depend on units; PES needs percentage changes.", "This is inverted; it puts price change on top instead of quantity supplied.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-116", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.5", "lv": 2, "q": "A straight-line supply curve starts at the origin and slopes upwards. What is its PES at every point?", "o": ["Exactly 1", "Infinity", "Greater than 1", "Zero"], "a": 0, "x": "Any straight-line supply curve through the origin has unit elasticity, because quantity changes in the same proportion as price. Steepness alone does not decide PES.", "w": ["", "Infinity means a horizontal supply curve, not an upward-sloping line.", "PES greater than 1 needs a straight supply line that cuts the price axis above the origin; a line from the origin gives exactly 1.", "Zero means perfectly inelastic, a vertical supply curve, not a ray from the origin."]}
{"id": "econ-117", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.5", "lv": 2, "q": "The price of a bakery's loaves rises from £2.00 to £2.40. Quantity supplied rises from 500 to 540 loaves a day. What is PES?", "o": ["1.25", "0.8", "0.4", "2.5"], "a": 2, "x": "%ΔQs = 40/500 = 8%. %ΔP = 0.40/2.00 = 20%. PES = 8 ÷ 20 = 0.4. Inverting gives 2.5, a common slip.", "w": ["This does not match 8% and 20%; it comes from a wrong base or inversion.", "Doubling the correct 0.4 is an arithmetic slip; 8 ÷ 20 is 0.4, not 0.8.", "", "This inverts the formula: 20 ÷ 8 = 2.5."]}
{"id": "econ-118", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.5", "lv": 2, "q": "A firm's PES for its product is 1.5. The price rises by 6%. By what percentage does quantity supplied rise?", "o": ["1.5%", "4%", "9%", "7.5%"], "a": 2, "x": "Rearrange: %ΔQs = PES × %ΔP = 1.5 × 6 = 9%. Dividing instead of multiplying is the usual slip.", "w": ["This just repeats the PES value rather than applying it to the price change.", "This divides 6 by 1.5, rearranging the formula wrongly.", "", "This adds 1.5 to 6, which is not how PES works."]}
{"id": "econ-119", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.5", "lv": 2, "q": "Which change would make a firm's supply of a good more price elastic?", "o": ["Production needs scarce, specialised workers", "The time period considered is shortened", "The firm holds large stocks of finished goods", "The firm is already at full capacity"], "a": 2, "x": "Large stocks let a firm raise output quickly when price rises, so supply is more elastic. Full capacity, scarce factors and a shorter time all make supply less elastic.", "w": ["Scarce, immobile factors make it harder to expand output, so supply is less elastic.", "Less time means firms cannot adjust inputs, making supply less elastic.", "", "At full capacity, output cannot rise easily, so supply is inelastic."]}
{"id": "econ-120", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.5", "lv": 3, "q": "Demand for strawberries rises, so price rises sharply in the short run. Which best explains why the price rise is smaller in the long run?", "o": ["Strawberries become a luxury good", "Producers' costs of production fall automatically", "Demand becomes more elastic over time", "Farmers can plant more, so supply is more elastic"], "a": 3, "x": "In the long run farmers can plant more and enter the market, so PES is higher. The same rise in demand then causes a larger rise in quantity and a smaller rise in price.", "w": ["Luxury status relates to income elasticity, not the time period of supply.", "Costs need not fall; the key is that factors become adjustable, raising PES.", "This confuses supply with demand; the question is about supply responding over time.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-121", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.6", "lv": 1, "q": "In a free market, the price is currently above the equilibrium price. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["Excess demand, so demand curve shifts right", "Excess supply, so price falls", "Excess supply, so supply curve shifts left", "Excess demand, so price rises"], "a": 1, "x": "Above equilibrium, quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded: excess supply. Unsold stock pushes price down, a movement along both curves. The trap is shifting curves when only price is changing.", "w": ["Excess demand is wrong here, and price changes do not shift demand.", "", "Price changes cause movements along curves, not shifts of the supply curve.", "Excess demand occurs below equilibrium, not above it."]}
{"id": "econ-122", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.6", "lv": 1, "q": "A rise in consumers' incomes increases demand for a normal good. What happens to equilibrium price and quantity?", "o": ["Price rises, quantity rises", "Price falls, quantity rises", "Price rises, quantity falls", "Price and quantity are unchanged"], "a": 0, "x": "Demand shifts right, creating excess demand at the old price. Price rises, causing supply to extend along the supply curve, so quantity also rises. The trap is forgetting the extension of supply.", "w": ["", "Price falls would need a rightward shift of supply, not demand.", "Quantity falls would need a leftward shift of demand or supply.", "Income is a non-price factor so it does shift demand and changes equilibrium."]}
{"id": "econ-123", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.6", "lv": 2, "q": "Demand: Qd = 120 − 4P. Supply: Qs = 20 + 6P. (P in £, Q in units.) What is the equilibrium price?", "o": ["£8", "£12", "£10", "£14"], "a": 2, "x": "Set Qd = Qs: 120 − 4P = 20 + 6P, so 100 = 10P and P = £10. Check: Q = 80 on both curves. The trap is mis-signing when collecting terms.", "w": ["At £8, Qd = 88 but Qs = 68, so it is not equilibrium.", "£12 gives Qd = 72 but Qs = 92, so it is not equilibrium.", "", "£14 gives Qd = 64 but Qs = 104, so it is not equilibrium."]}
{"id": "econ-124", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.6", "lv": 2, "q": "Using Qd = 120 − 4P and Qs = 20 + 6P, the price is fixed at £6. What is the market outcome?", "o": ["Excess demand of 40 units", "Excess supply of 20 units", "Excess demand of 20 units", "Excess supply of 40 units"], "a": 0, "x": "At £6, Qd = 120 − 24 = 96 and Qs = 20 + 36 = 56. Qd exceeds Qs by 40, so excess demand of 40. The trap is mixing up the two quantities.", "w": ["", "Price £6 is below equilibrium £10, so it is excess demand, and the size is wrong.", "Direction is right but 20 is not Qd − Qs at £6, which is 40.", "Price is below equilibrium, so there is excess demand, not supply."]}
{"id": "econ-125", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.6", "lv": 2, "q": "A new tax on producers shifts supply left for a good with a downward-sloping demand curve. Compared with before, what happens to equilibrium?", "o": ["Price rises, quantity falls", "Price falls, quantity falls", "Price falls, quantity rises", "Price rises, quantity rises"], "a": 0, "x": "Supply shifts left, so at the old price there is excess demand. Price rises, and demand contracts along its curve, so quantity falls. The trap is thinking quantity rises with price.", "w": ["", "Falling price would need a rightward shift of supply, not leftward.", "This describes a rightward supply shift, the opposite of a tax.", "Demand has not shifted, so higher price means contraction in quantity, not a rise."]}
{"id": "econ-126", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.6", "lv": 3, "q": "Demand and supply of a good both shift right, with the demand shift much larger. What is certain about the new equilibrium compared with the old?", "o": ["Quantity is higher, price is uncertain", "Price is lower and quantity is higher", "Price is higher, quantity is uncertain", "Price is higher and quantity is higher"], "a": 3, "x": "Both shifts raise quantity. Price rises because the larger demand shift outweighs the supply shift. If sizes were unknown, price would be uncertain; here demand shifts more, so price rises.", "w": ["Price is determined by the larger shift, which is demand, so it is not uncertain.", "A lower price needs the supply shift to be larger than demand's.", "Quantity rises with both rightward shifts, so it is not uncertain.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-127", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.7", "lv": 1, "q": "In a free market, what is meant by the rationing function of the price mechanism?", "o": ["Price falls so firms are encouraged to produce more of the good", "Price changes tell firms which goods consumers will want in future", "Price rises to limit demand when a good is scarce, so only those willing and able to pay buy it", "The government shares out goods using coupons so everyone gets an equal amount"], "a": 2, "x": "Rationing means a higher price allocates scarce goods to those willing and able to pay, reducing quantity demanded. A falling price that cuts supply incentives is not rationing, and telling firms what consumers want is signalling.", "w": ["A falling price would reduce supply incentives; this misreads the incentive function and the direction of price change.", "This describes the signalling function, not rationing; the functions are often confused.", "", "Coupon rationing is government allocation, not the price mechanism; confuses rationing with administered distribution."]}
{"id": "econ-128", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.7", "lv": 1, "q": "A sudden rise in demand for electric bikes raises their price and profits. Which function of the price mechanism encourages firms to supply more?", "o": ["Redistribution", "Rationing", "Fixed pricing", "Incentive"], "a": 3, "x": "Higher prices and profit give firms an incentive to produce more. Rationing restricts demand, not encourages supply. Redistribution is not a price mechanism function.", "w": ["Redistribution is a government aim using tax and benefits, not a function of the price mechanism.", "Rationing limits demand among buyers; it does not explain why firms increase output.", "Fixed prices would stop prices responding; the price mechanism relies on changing prices.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-129", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.7", "lv": 2, "q": "Demand for oat milk rises, shifting the demand curve right. Which sequence best shows the signalling function at work?", "o": ["Price rises, so supply shifts left as costs increase", "Price rises, signalling to producers to move resources into oat milk", "Price rises, so consumers buy less and the market clears", "Price rises, so the government subsidises oat milk"], "a": 1, "x": "Signalling: the higher price (and profit) tells producers that consumers want more oat milk, so resources move into it. Buying less until the market clears is rationing; students often mix the two functions.", "w": ["Supply does not shift left; higher price leads to an extension of supply.", "", "This describes rationing, a reduced quantity demanded, not a message to producers.", "Government subsidy is intervention, not the price mechanism working freely."]}
{"id": "econ-130", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.7", "lv": 2, "q": "Heavy snow cuts the supply of fresh vegetables in a UK town. Prices rise. Which statement correctly describes the effect?", "o": ["Higher prices remove the need for any producers to respond", "Higher prices mean excess demand remains permanently", "Higher prices ration the limited stock to buyers willing and able to pay, moving to a new equilibrium", "Higher prices increase demand, shifting the demand curve right"], "a": 2, "x": "The supply curve shifts left, creating excess demand at the old price. Price rises and quantity demanded contracts along the demand curve, rationing the scarce stock. Demand does not shift because of price.", "w": ["Higher prices give an incentive for suppliers elsewhere to send more vegetables.", "The market moves to a new equilibrium as price rises, so excess demand is removed.", "", "A price change causes a movement along demand, not a shift; confusing movement with shift."]}
{"id": "econ-131", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.7", "lv": 2, "q": "Tastes move away from diesel cars. Demand falls, price falls and profits fall. What does the price mechanism signal to producers?", "o": ["Increase diesel production to restore price", "Raise prices to protect profit", "Keep output unchanged as price will rise", "Reduce diesel output and move resources to alternatives in higher demand"], "a": 3, "x": "Falling price and profit signal that consumers want less, so resources leave diesel cars for more profitable uses. Increasing output would waste resources. Raising price ignores the signal.", "w": ["Expanding output when demand falls ignores the signal and would lower price further.", "Firms in a competitive market cannot simply raise price against falling demand; this ignores the signal.", "Waiting contradicts the signal; falling profit gives incentive to reallocate now.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-132", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.7", "lv": 3, "q": "Concert tickets are priced below equilibrium, creating excess demand and queues. Which statement is correct about the price mechanism here?", "o": ["The price is not rationing by willingness to pay: demand exceeds supply, so allocation is by queue or luck", "Producers have a stronger incentive to expand supply at this price", "Price is rationing efficiently because tickets go to those who value them most", "Excess demand sends a signal to lower the price further"], "a": 0, "x": "Below equilibrium, price does not ration by willingness to pay; allocation is by queue or luck. The signal to raise price is not acted on. Producers' incentive is weaker than at equilibrium.", "w": ["", "A low price gives less profit incentive, not more, so supply incentives are weaker.", "Allocation by queue need not favour highest valuation; price is not doing the rationing.", "Excess demand signals price should rise, not fall; direction error."]}
{"id": "econ-133", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.8", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement correctly defines consumer surplus?", "o": ["The difference between what consumers are willing to pay and what they actually pay", "The total amount consumers spend on a good in a market", "The difference between the price paid and the cost of producing the good", "The extra revenue firms gain when price rises above equilibrium"], "a": 0, "x": "Consumer surplus is the gap between willingness to pay (shown by the demand curve) and the price actually paid. Confusing it with the price-minus-cost gap mixes it up with producer surplus.", "w": ["", "This is consumer expenditure (P × Q), not surplus; surplus is the extra benefit above what is paid.", "This describes producer-style profit margin, not consumer surplus; consumer surplus uses willingness to pay.", "This describes a producer gain, not consumer surplus, and consumers do not receive revenue."]}
{"id": "econ-134", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.8", "lv": 1, "q": "On a standard supply and demand diagram, producer surplus at the equilibrium price is shown by the area:", "o": ["below the supply curve, down to the quantity axis", "above the supply curve and below the equilibrium price, up to equilibrium quantity", "above the demand curve and below the equilibrium price", "between the supply and demand curves beyond equilibrium quantity"], "a": 1, "x": "Producer surplus is the gap between the price received and the minimum price producers would accept (supply curve), up to equilibrium output. Mixing it up with total cost, the area below supply, is the slip.", "w": ["The area under supply shows total variable cost of production, not surplus.", "", "The area above demand and below price is not a surplus; consumer surplus lies under demand and above price.", "Beyond equilibrium quantity, supply lies above demand, so no surplus is earned on those units."]}
{"id": "econ-135", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.8", "lv": 2, "q": "Demand is P = 20 − 2Q and supply is P = 2 + Q, with P in £ and Q in units. What is consumer surplus at equilibrium?", "o": ["£72", "£36", "£60", "£18"], "a": 1, "x": "Equilibrium: 20 − 2Q = 2 + Q, so Q = 6 and P = £8. Consumer surplus = ½ × 6 × (20 − 8) = £36. Using 20 as the height, or omitting the ½, is the trap.", "w": ["This forgets the ½ for a triangle: 6 × 12 = 72.", "", "This uses ½ × 6 × 20, taking the full intercept as the height instead of the gap 20 − 8.", "This is producer surplus, ½ × 6 × (8 − 2), not consumer surplus."]}
{"id": "econ-136", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.8", "lv": 2, "q": "Demand for a UK product rises, shifting the demand curve to the right. Supply is upward-sloping and unchanged. Which is certain to happen?", "o": ["Consumer surplus falls because the equilibrium price rises", "Producer surplus rises because price and quantity both rise", "Producer surplus is unchanged because the supply curve has not shifted", "Producer surplus falls because firms must produce more units"], "a": 1, "x": "Higher price and higher quantity widen the area above supply and below price, so producer surplus rises. Thinking surplus only changes when supply shifts is the slip; movement along supply changes the area.", "w": ["The demand curve moves out too, so consumer surplus is not certain to fall just because price rises.", "", "The area depends on price and quantity, which change, even though the curve itself does not shift.", "More units at a higher price enlarge producer surplus; output rising does not shrink it."]}
{"id": "econ-137", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.8", "lv": 2, "q": "Demand is P = 30 − 3Q. New technology shifts supply right, so price falls from £15 to £9 and quantity rises from 5 to 7. What is the change in consumer surplus?", "o": ["It rises by £73.50", "It rises by £36", "It rises by £18", "It falls by £36"], "a": 1, "x": "Before: ½ × 5 × 15 = £37.50. After: ½ × 7 × (30 − 9) = £73.50. The change is +£36. Giving the new level instead of the change is the slip.", "w": ["£73.50 is the new consumer surplus, not the change from the old level.", "", "This halves the change again; the £36 difference is already the difference between two triangle areas.", "Cheaper price with more sold enlarges the triangle, so consumer surplus rises, not falls."]}
{"id": "econ-138", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.8", "lv": 3, "q": "Demand is P = 20 − 2Q, supply P = 2 + Q (£, units). A minimum price of £10 cuts sales to 5 units. Producers sell all 5; the 5 units go to the highest-value buyers. Change in total surplus?", "o": ["Falls by £11, the loss of consumer surplus", "Falls by £1.50", "Rises by £9.50, as producers gain", "No change, as surplus is only transferred"], "a": 1, "x": "Before: CS £36 + PS £18 = £54. After: CS ½ × 5 × 10 = £25; PS = ½ × 5 × 5 + 5 × 3 = £27.50; total £52.50. Total falls £1.50. Ignoring the lost trade is the trap.", "w": ["£11 is the consumer surplus loss only; producers gain £9.50, so the net change is smaller.", "", "£9.50 is the rise in producer surplus alone; consumers lose £11, so the total does not rise.", "Some surplus moves to producers, but trades between 5 and 6 units are lost, so the total falls."]}
{"id": "econ-139", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.10", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement best describes the traditional economic assumption that behavioural economics challenges?", "o": ["Consumers only buy goods with price-elastic demand", "Consumers have unlimited incomes to spend", "Consumers never respond to changes in price", "Consumers always act rationally to maximise their utility"], "a": 3, "x": "Traditional theory assumes rational consumers who maximise utility using full information. Behavioural economics shows that social influences, habits and limited calculation ability can lead people away from this. Unlimited income is not assumed.", "w": ["Wrong: elasticity describes responsiveness and is not an assumption about how consumers decide.", "Wrong: consumers are assumed to face a budget constraint, so this confuses rationality with income.", "Wrong: traditional theory says price changes do affect demand, so this misstates the assumption.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-140", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.10", "lv": 1, "q": "A teenager buys a particular trainer brand only because most of her friends have bought it. Which concept from behavioural economics does this best illustrate?", "o": ["Herd behaviour", "Rule of thumb", "Perfect information", "Habitual behaviour"], "a": 0, "x": "Copying the choices of others is herd behaviour, where social influence overrides personal evaluation of costs and benefits. Habit means repeating your own past choices, not following a group.", "w": ["", "Wrong: a rule of thumb is a simple shortcut for decisions, not copying a group.", "Wrong: she lacks independent information, so perfect information is the opposite of the case.", "Wrong: habit comes from repeating your own past purchases, not from copying friends."]}
{"id": "econ-141", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.10", "lv": 1, "q": "A commuter buys the same brand of coffee from the same café every morning without comparing prices. This is best described as:", "o": ["Habitual behaviour", "Bounded rationality caused by social pressure", "Utility maximisation with full information", "Herd behaviour"], "a": 0, "x": "Repeating a past choice automatically, without weighing up alternatives, is habitual behaviour. It saves effort but means the consumer may miss cheaper options, so demand responds less to price changes.", "w": ["", "Wrong: no social pressure is described, only repetition of her own routine.", "Wrong: she does not compare prices, so she is not weighing up all information.", "Wrong: no one else's choices are influencing her; herd behaviour needs copying others."]}
{"id": "econ-142", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.10", "lv": 2, "q": "Many customers choose a mobile phone contract by comparing only the monthly fee, ignoring data limits and handset costs. Which is the best explanation of this behaviour?", "o": ["They are maximising utility with full information", "They are following a long-held habit", "They are copying the choices of other consumers", "They find complex calculations difficult, so use a simple shortcut"], "a": 3, "x": "Comparing total cost and value across many features is hard, so consumers rely on a rule of thumb such as the monthly fee. This is difficulty with calculations, not herd behaviour or habit.", "w": ["Wrong: ignoring key features means information is not being fully used.", "Wrong: nothing suggests repeating past choices; the issue is the difficulty of comparing.", "Wrong: no copying of others is described, only a simplified comparison.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-143", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.10", "lv": 2, "q": "A new restaurant has a long queue. Passers-by join it without checking reviews or the menu. What is the likely effect on the restaurant's demand, and why?", "o": ["Demand is unchanged because consumers are rational", "Demand falls because queues raise the time cost", "Demand rises as herd behaviour attracts more customers", "Demand falls because habit makes consumers loyal elsewhere"], "a": 2, "x": "People infer quality from others' choices, so the queue itself attracts more customers. Herd behaviour can raise demand beyond what quality alone would justify, shifting the demand curve right.", "w": ["Wrong: the passers-by skip checking information, so they are not acting rationally.", "Wrong: this applies rational time-cost reasoning, but the scenario shows people copying others.", "", "Wrong: habit would keep them at other places, but they are choosing the new one because of the queue."]}
{"id": "econ-144", "s": "micro", "t": "1.2.10", "lv": 3, "q": "Energy customers rarely switch supplier even when a cheaper tariff exists, leaving many on the standard variable tariff. Which explanation draws on two behavioural factors?", "o": ["Perfect information and herd behaviour", "Utility maximisation and social norms", "Habit and difficulty comparing complex tariffs", "Rational choice and a lack of substitutes"], "a": 2, "x": "Staying with the same supplier is habit, and complex tariffs make comparison hard. Together they explain inertia and weaker price responsiveness. Rational choice would mean switching to the cheaper tariff.", "w": ["Wrong: customers lack full information, and herd behaviour would involve copying others, not staying put.", "Wrong: utility maximisation would lead to switching, so it cannot explain inertia.", "", "Wrong: rational choice contradicts the behaviour, and many substitute suppliers exist."]}
{"id": "econ-145", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.1", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement best defines market failure?", "o": ["Firms in a market make a loss and leave the industry", "Prices in a market rise because of a fall in supply", "The free market allocates resources inefficiently, so social welfare is not maximised", "The government has to provide a good or service"], "a": 2, "x": "Market failure means the market misallocates resources, so society's welfare is not maximised. Falling profits, price rises or state provision are not the definition; the trap is confusing failure of firms with failure of the market.", "w": ["Firms leaving after losses is normal market adjustment, not misallocation; this confuses business failure with market failure.", "A price rise from a supply fall is just a market adjusting; it does not show misallocation.", "", "State provision may be a response to failure, not its definition; this confuses cause with remedy."]}
{"id": "econ-146", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.1", "lv": 1, "q": "A good is non-rival and non-excludable. Which problem does this create for a private firm?", "o": ["It must charge a price equal to marginal cost", "It cannot charge a price because it cannot stop non-payers using the good", "It cannot produce the good because demand is zero", "It faces a negative externality in consumption"], "a": 1, "x": "Non-excludability means people can free-ride, so the firm cannot make them pay. Demand exists, but is not revealed through payment, so the private market under-provides or does not provide the good.", "w": ["Marginal cost pricing is not the issue; with no payment possible, there is no price to set.", "", "Demand exists, since people value the good; the problem is collecting payment, not zero demand.", "Free-riding is about non-payment, not a negative externality; this mixes up the two types of failure."]}
{"id": "econ-147", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.1", "lv": 2, "q": "A factory's production has a marginal private cost of £8 per unit and causes pollution costing neighbours £3 per unit. What is the marginal social cost per unit?", "o": ["£11", "£3", "£5", "£24"], "a": 0, "x": "MSC = MPC + marginal external cost = £8 + £3 = £11. The trap is to omit the private cost, or to subtract the external cost instead of adding it.", "w": ["", "£3 is only the external cost; this forgets to include the firm's own private cost.", "£5 subtracts the external cost; external costs add to private costs.", "£24 multiplies the figures; social cost is found by adding, not multiplying."]}
{"id": "econ-148", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Smokers underestimate the health harm of smoking. In market failure terms, which is the best description of this problem?", "o": ["Asymmetric information, so consumers do not know the true cost", "A monopoly that restricts output", "A positive externality in production", "A public good that is under-provided"], "a": 0, "x": "Consumers lack full information about the harm, an information gap, so they overconsume. It is not a public good or monopoly issue. The trap is labelling any harmful good as an externality without checking the cause.", "w": ["", "Nothing in the question concerns market power; this guesses a failure unrelated to the information problem.", "Externalities in production concern third parties with benefits; here the issue is the buyer's own misjudgement.", "Cigarettes are rival and excludable, so they are not public goods; this mixes up the types of failure."]}
{"id": "econ-149", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.1", "lv": 2, "q": "A town's private bus firms run a service with marginal private benefit (MPB) of £6 per journey. Each journey also gives £2 of benefit to local shops. Which is correct?", "o": ["MSB is £8 and the good is under-consumed by the free market", "MSB is £6 because only buyers' benefits count", "MSB is £8 and the good is over-consumed", "MSB is £4 and the good is over-consumed"], "a": 0, "x": "MSB = MPB + marginal external benefit = £6 + £2 = £8. Consumers ignore the £2 benefit to others, so the market consumes less than the social optimum: under-consumption, a positive externality of consumption.", "w": ["", "Social benefit includes third-party benefits; ignoring them is exactly the market's own mistake.", "The sum is right, but positive externalities lead to under-consumption, not over-consumption.", "Subtracting the £2 treats the benefit as a cost; positive externalities add to private benefit."]}
{"id": "econ-150", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.1", "lv": 3, "q": "A street light lights a road for all. Which pair of reasons best explains why the free market would fail to provide it efficiently?", "o": ["It has a high price elasticity of demand, so profits are low", "It is rival and excludable, so firms overcharge", "It is non-rival and non-excludable, so free riders prevent revenue being earned", "It is a demerit good, so consumers overvalue it"], "a": 2, "x": "A street light is a pure public good: one person's use does not reduce another's (non-rival) and nobody can be stopped from using it (non-excludable). Free riding means no revenue, so private firms do not supply it.", "w": ["Elasticity is irrelevant to the missing market; this brings in an unrelated concept.", "Street lights are not rival or excludable; this describes a private good and invents overcharging.", "", "Demerit goods are overconsumed because of misjudged harm; a street light is not harmful, so this misapplies the concept."]}
{"id": "econ-151", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which pair of characteristics defines a pure public good?", "o": ["Rival and non-excludable", "Non-rival and non-excludable", "Non-rival and excludable", "Provided by government and free at the point of use"], "a": 1, "x": "A pure public good is non-rival (one person's consumption doesn't reduce another's) and non-excludable (non-payers can't be stopped). Government provision is a policy response, not part of the definition.", "w": ["Rival and non-excludable describes a common access resource such as an open-access fishery, not a public good.", "", "Non-rival but excludable describes a quasi-public or club good, such as a subscription service.", "Government provision and zero price are consequences of the market failure, not defining characteristics."]}
{"id": "econ-152", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.3", "lv": 1, "q": "A free rider is best described as a consumer who:", "o": ["Receives a good free from the government", "Pays less than others for the same good", "Consumes more than the socially optimal amount", "Benefits from a good without paying for it"], "a": 3, "x": "Free riders enjoy a non-excludable good without paying. Because they can't be stopped, firms can't collect revenue from them, which undermines private provision.", "w": ["This confuses free riding with state provision; free riders avoid paying when they could be asked to.", "Paying less is price discrimination, not free riding; a free rider pays nothing.", "Over-consumption describes demerit goods or negative consumption externalities, not free riding.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-153", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.3", "lv": 1, "q": "Which is the best example of a pure public good in the UK?", "o": ["Public library book loans", "National defence", "A state secondary school", "A toll motorway"], "a": 1, "x": "Defence protects everyone in the country at once (non-rival) and nobody can be excluded. Schools and libraries are rival or excludable, and tolls exclude non-payers. Public funding does not make a good public.", "w": ["Books borrowed are rival, since a borrowed copy can't be used by others at the same time.", "", "Schools are rival (class places are limited) and excludable, so they are publicly provided merit goods.", "A toll excludes non-payers, so it is excludable and not a pure public good."]}
{"id": "econ-154", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.3", "lv": 2, "q": "Why will a private firm usually fail to provide street lighting for a town?", "o": ["Marginal cost is too high because each extra user adds to cost", "Consumers value street lighting too little to want it", "Demand is perfectly price inelastic, so profit is guaranteed", "It cannot exclude non-payers, so revenue is too low to cover costs"], "a": 3, "x": "Non-excludability lets people use the lighting without paying, so the firm can't collect enough revenue. The market fails to provide it despite real demand. Marginal cost of an extra user is zero, not high.", "w": ["Non-rivalry means the marginal cost of an extra user is zero, not high. The problem is revenue, not cost.", "People do value it; the free-rider problem hides this demand, so effective demand is understated.", "Free riding means little or no revenue is collected, so profit isn't guaranteed.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-155", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.3", "lv": 2, "q": "A lighthouse's beam helps all ships nearby. Ship owners each hope others will pay for it. What does this show about the market?", "o": ["Government failure caused by too much regulation", "Missing market: no price signal, so the good is under-provided", "Allocative efficiency, since benefit is shared widely", "Over-provision, since ships benefit without paying"], "a": 1, "x": "Ship owners free ride, so revealed demand is far below true social benefit. No market forms, so provision is zero or too low. This is complete market failure, not over-provision.", "w": ["No government intervention is involved; the failure arises from the good's characteristics in a free market.", "", "Allocative efficiency needs P = MC with true benefits revealed; here a missing market means output is too low.", "Free riding reduces payments and so provision; it leads to under-provision, not over."]}
{"id": "econ-156", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.3", "lv": 3, "q": "A council pays for a park with no entry gates, funded by council tax. Which statement is correct?", "o": ["It is excludable because council tax payers fund it", "Provision by the council makes it a pure public good", "It is nearly non-excludable and non-rival, though crowding could make it rival", "It is rival because the council employs staff"], "a": 2, "x": "Without gates, non-payers can't be excluded, and use is mostly non-rival until crowded. Who pays or provides doesn't define a public good; the characteristics do. Tax funding overcomes free riding.", "w": ["Funding by council tax is not exclusion from use; non-payers can still enter.", "Public provision is not the test; characteristics of the good decide whether it is a public good.", "", "Employing staff is irrelevant to rivalry; rivalry is about one person's use reducing others'."]}
{"id": "econ-157", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.4", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement best describes asymmetric information?", "o": ["Information is provided free by the government to all parties", "One party to a transaction has more relevant information than the other", "Buyers and sellers both have equal but incomplete knowledge", "Consumers have no information about any product at all"], "a": 1, "x": "Asymmetric information means unequal knowledge between buyer and seller. Equal but incomplete knowledge is symmetric information, a common confusion with 'imperfect'.", "w": ["This describes a possible remedy, not the definition of the information gap.", "", "This is symmetric information: both sides are equally informed, even if neither knows everything.", "Asymmetry is about unequal knowledge, not total absence of knowledge on both sides."]}
{"id": "econ-158", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.4", "lv": 1, "q": "A second-hand car seller knows the car has a serious fault; the buyer cannot tell. Who has the information advantage, and what is this an example of?", "o": ["The buyer; symmetric information", "The seller; asymmetric information", "The seller; a positive externality", "The buyer; asymmetric information"], "a": 1, "x": "The seller knows more than the buyer, so information is asymmetric. Hidden quality is an information failure, not an externality, which concerns third parties.", "w": ["The buyer lacks knowledge, and symmetric would mean equal knowledge.", "", "Externalities affect third parties; this is a gap between the two parties themselves.", "The buyer is the less informed party here, so has no advantage."]}
{"id": "econ-159", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.4", "lv": 2, "q": "Consumers underestimate the long-term health harm of a sugary drink. Compared with the socially optimal level, what is likely to happen to consumption and why?", "o": ["Underconsumption, because price is too high", "Underconsumption, because private benefit is below social benefit", "Optimal consumption, because consumers choose freely", "Overconsumption, because perceived private benefit exceeds true benefit"], "a": 3, "x": "Consumers overestimate the benefit (or underestimate the cost), so demand is too high and the good is overconsumed. Free choice only gives efficiency when choices are well informed.", "w": ["Price is not the issue; misperceived benefits push demand up, not down.", "That fits a merit good where benefits are underestimated, the reverse of this case.", "Free choice is efficient only with full information; here the information gap distorts demand.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-160", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.4", "lv": 2, "q": "A health insurer cannot observe how risky each applicant is, so it charges one average premium. Low-risk people leave and the average risk of those insured rises. This is best called:", "o": ["A positive externality", "Adverse selection", "Price discrimination", "Moral hazard"], "a": 1, "x": "Adverse selection arises before the contract when hidden information about risk means the 'worse' risks remain in the market. Moral hazard is changed behaviour after being insured.", "w": ["No third-party spillover is described; the problem is hidden risk information.", "", "Price discrimination means charging different prices to different groups, but here one price is charged.", "Moral hazard is riskier behaviour after taking cover, not who chooses to buy it."]}
{"id": "econ-161", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.4", "lv": 2, "q": "Which policy is most directly aimed at reducing an information gap in the market for processed food?", "o": ["A maximum price on food", "Compulsory nutritional labelling on packaging", "A per-unit tax on all food", "A subsidy to food producers"], "a": 1, "x": "Compulsory labelling gives consumers the information needed to choose well, reducing asymmetry. Taxes, price controls and subsidies alter prices but do not tackle the lack of information itself.", "w": ["A price ceiling creates shortages and does nothing for consumer knowledge.", "", "A tax changes price, so it addresses externalities more directly than missing information.", "A subsidy lowers costs but leaves the information gap unchanged."]}
{"id": "econ-162", "s": "micro", "t": "1.3.4", "lv": 3, "q": "Evaluating compulsory labelling to correct information failure, which is the strongest limitation?", "o": ["Consumers may ignore or misunderstand labels, and compliance has costs", "It always eliminates overconsumption", "It raises the price elasticity of supply to infinity", "It makes information asymmetric"], "a": 0, "x": "Labelling only works if consumers notice, understand and act on it, and firms bear compliance costs passed on in prices. It cannot guarantee a fully optimal outcome.", "w": ["", "That overstates its effect; it is a claimed strength, not a limitation.", "This is not a plausible effect and has no link to information.", "Labelling reduces asymmetry rather than creating it."]}
{"id": "econ-163", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.1", "lv": 1, "q": "A government imposes a specific tax on a good. What happens to the supply curve?", "o": ["It shifts left by a growing amount as price rises", "It shifts left by the amount of the tax at every quantity (parallel shift)", "It pivots, getting steeper as price rises", "It shifts right by the amount of the tax"], "a": 1, "x": "A specific tax is a fixed £ amount per unit, so supply shifts vertically up (left) by that amount: a parallel shift. An ad valorem tax would pivot the curve. Mixing the two is the trap.", "w": ["Growing gaps describe ad valorem tax; a specific tax is the same £ per unit everywhere.", "", "Pivoting describes an ad valorem tax, which is a percentage of price, not a specific tax.", "A shift right is what a subsidy does, not a tax; taxes raise costs."]}
{"id": "econ-164", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.1", "lv": 1, "q": "A maximum price is set below the free-market equilibrium price. What is the result?", "o": ["No effect, because the price is already below equilibrium", "A fall in demand until the market clears", "Excess demand (a shortage), because quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied", "A surplus, because firms produce more at the lower price"], "a": 2, "x": "A binding maximum price below equilibrium raises quantity demanded and cuts quantity supplied, creating excess demand. Students often confuse this with a minimum price, which creates a surplus.", "w": ["A cap below equilibrium is binding, so it does affect the market.", "Demand does not shift; the price is held down so the market cannot clear.", "", "Lower prices reduce quantity supplied, so a surplus is wrong; that confuses it with a minimum price."]}
{"id": "econ-165", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.1", "lv": 2, "q": "The market for a good has equilibrium at £12. A £3 specific tax is imposed and the new equilibrium price paid by consumers is £14. How much of the tax is borne by producers?", "o": ["£3", "£1", "£2", "£14"], "a": 1, "x": "Consumers pay £2 more (£14 − £12), so they bear £2 of the £3 tax. Producers bear the remainder: £3 − £2 = £1. The trap is reading the price rise as the producers' share.", "w": ["£3 is the whole tax; the burden is shared because the price rose by less than £3.", "", "£2 is the consumers' burden; the slip is giving the consumer share as the producer share.", "£14 is the new market price, not a share of the tax."]}
{"id": "econ-166", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.1", "lv": 2, "q": "The government sets a minimum price above equilibrium in a market. Which outcome is most likely?", "o": ["A fall in the price paid by consumers", "Excess demand and queues", "A rise in quantity demanded", "Excess supply, which the government may have to buy up or tolerate as waste"], "a": 3, "x": "A minimum price above equilibrium raises quantity supplied and reduces quantity demanded, creating excess supply. Producers gain per unit, but surplus stock may need buying. Confusing it with a maximum price gives queues.", "w": ["A minimum price above equilibrium raises the price consumers pay.", "Excess demand arises from a maximum price below equilibrium, not a minimum price.", "Higher prices reduce quantity demanded along the demand curve, not raise it.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-167", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.1", "lv": 2, "q": "Which is the best reason a tradable permit scheme may reduce pollution efficiently?", "o": ["Firms with low abatement costs cut pollution and sell permits to those with high costs", "It gives firms free information about pollution", "It sets a tax equal to the marginal social cost", "It bans all firms from polluting"], "a": 0, "x": "Permits cap total pollution. Trading lets firms with cheaper abatement cut more and sell spare permits, so the cut is made where cost is lowest. It is not a ban or a tax.", "w": ["", "Information provision is a separate intervention; permits work through a capped, tradable quantity.", "That describes a tax on pollution, a different tool from permits.", "Permits allow pollution up to a cap, so it is not a ban."]}
{"id": "econ-168", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.1", "lv": 3, "q": "A good has highly inelastic demand and elastic supply. A per-unit tax is imposed. Which statement is most accurate?", "o": ["Consumers bear none of the tax because supply is elastic", "Producers bear most of the tax, so price barely changes", "The tax is shared equally whatever the elasticities", "Consumers bear most of the tax, and the quantity traded falls only slightly"], "a": 3, "x": "Inelastic demand means buyers cannot easily cut back, so the price rises by nearly the full tax and they bear most of it. Quantity falls little. Reversing the elasticities is the common slip.", "w": ["Elastic supply shifts the burden onto consumers when demand is inelastic, not away from them.", "That fits elastic demand and inelastic supply; the elasticities are reversed.", "Incidence depends on relative elasticities, so it is not equal.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-169", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which statement best defines government failure?", "o": ["Intervention that reduces welfare, giving a net welfare loss compared with no intervention", "A policy that raises the government's budget deficit", "A policy that fails to meet its stated target", "Any market where the government has to step in to correct a problem"], "a": 0, "x": "Government failure is intervention that causes a net welfare loss, so resources are allocated less efficiently than without it. Missing a target is not enough; the outcome must be worse than before.", "w": ["", "Deficits are a fiscal issue; they do not by themselves show a welfare loss in the market.", "Missing a target is not necessarily a net welfare loss; this confuses effectiveness with efficiency.", "That describes market failure, the reason for intervening, not government failure."]}
{"id": "econ-170", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.2", "lv": 1, "q": "A government sets a maximum price below the equilibrium price in a private rental market. Which is a likely unintended consequence?", "o": ["A surplus of rental housing", "A fall in the demand for rented homes", "A rise in the quantity of rental housing supplied", "Excess demand, with the quantity supplied falling"], "a": 3, "x": "A maximum price below equilibrium lowers quantity supplied and raises quantity demanded, creating excess demand (a shortage). A surplus comes from a minimum price above equilibrium.", "w": ["Surplus arises with a minimum price above equilibrium; this mixes up the two price controls.", "Quantity demanded rises along the curve at the lower price; it does not fall.", "Quantity supplied contracts along the supply curve as price falls, it does not rise.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-171", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.2", "lv": 1, "q": "Which is an example of regulatory capture?", "o": ["A regulator sets a price cap that is too low for firms", "A regulator is replaced after a change of government", "A regulator fines a firm for breaking competition law", "A regulator ends up acting in the interests of the firms it regulates"], "a": 3, "x": "Regulatory capture occurs when a regulator, influenced by the industry it oversees, serves producers' interests rather than consumers'. It is a source of government failure.", "w": ["A badly set cap is a different cause, poor information, not capture by the industry.", "A change of personnel is not capture; capture needs influence by the regulated firms.", "Fining a law-breaker is the regulator doing its job, not being captured.", ""]}
{"id": "econ-172", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.2", "lv": 2, "q": "The government imposes an indirect tax on a good with a negative externality, but sets it at £6 per unit when the marginal external cost is £4 per unit. What is the likely effect?", "o": ["There is no change because tax and external cost differ", "Efficiency is restored because output falls", "Output falls below the social optimum, creating a welfare loss", "Output rises above the social optimum"], "a": 2, "x": "A tax above the marginal external cost cuts output below the social optimum, where marginal social benefit exceeds marginal social cost on the lost units. That is a welfare loss: government failure.", "w": ["The tax still shifts supply, so output changes; the mismatch causes the loss.", "Output falls too far, not just to the optimum; a tax of £4 would restore efficiency.", "", "Tax shifts supply left, so output falls; it cannot rise above the optimum."]}
{"id": "econ-173", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.2", "lv": 2, "q": "A government subsidises a farm product, which leads to persistent overproduction that is bought up and stored. Which cause of government failure is this best an example of?", "o": ["Regulatory capture", "Distortion of price signals and unintended consequences", "Lack of any externality in the market", "Excessive administrative costs only"], "a": 1, "x": "The subsidy distorts price signals, so farmers produce beyond what consumers value, and surpluses emerge as an unintended consequence. Resources are misallocated, giving a welfare loss.", "w": ["No sign that the industry controls the regulator; this is not capture.", "", "The issue is the policy's effect on output, not whether an externality exists.", "Storage costs are admin-related, but the core problem is the distorted incentive to overproduce."]}
{"id": "econ-174", "s": "micro", "t": "1.4.2", "lv": 3, "q": "A tax set above the marginal external cost cuts output below the social optimum, creating a welfare loss triangle. The tax is then reduced so output rises towards the optimum. What happens to the welfare loss?", "o": ["It rises because more is produced", "It shrinks as output moves towards the social optimum", "It stays the same because the tax still exists", "It grows because the tax is lower"], "a": 1, "x": "Welfare loss is the area between marginal social benefit and cost over units lost relative to the optimum. Moving output towards the optimum shrinks the triangle; it vanishes at the optimum.", "w": ["More output is not automatically worse; here it moves towards the optimum, so the loss falls.", "", "The loss depends on how far output is from the optimum, not whether a tax exists.", "A lower tax moves output closer to the optimum, so the loss falls; this reverses the link."]}
